hello everyone I'm Jesse and welcome to  my kitchen today I'm going to share some  ways with you that you can save money by  re growing your own veggies at home  using food scraps so the first one that  I want to share with you is lettuce a  really easy way to regrow what you want  to do with your lettuce is when you buy  your iceberg lettuce you can cut about  an inch the two inches off the bottom  part that will be where the roots will  grow and basically you just want to put  it in a container small container of  water to where it does not fill all the  way about halfway is pretty good you  just basically change out your water  every day and you will start to see this  grow you don't even need to put any soil  or in it for the first few days  basically I've had this one for about  six days now and I've just been changing  out the water and you can already see  it's got about three to four inches of  growth so at this point now now that it  has established growth I can actually  take it outside and plant it or if you  choose to have it inside you can just  basically wait until it grows a little  bit longer and eat it from there now the  thing about that is it will not  reproduce as in if you cut it that part  it's not going to continuously be an  endless supply so you will want to plant  it if you want it to keep growing the  next one that I want to share with you  is basically the same thing except  obviously a different plant and it is  celery I've done the same thing with the  celery I did it about at the same time  small container again fill it about half  way with water and you can start to see  little buds growing I don't know if  they're called buds or what they're  called but basically it's starting to  have new growth here the celery has  grown a little bit slower but I can do  the same thing as with the lettuce once  it starts to establish growth or you see  roots growing you can take it outside  and plant it from there or keep it  inside make sure you just keep changing  the water on a daily basis the next one  that I want to share with you is garlic  it is so easy to grow and I've done it  here at my house I didn't have a garden  or an extra planter so I just use my  aloe and planted it right next to  and we'll have to replant it later  because they're watering schedule is  different but it's so easy you basically  take a garlic clove especially the ones  that you start to see growth in like  such as this one you can take the root  and towards the bottom part of your  planter plant it halfway so that part of  it exposed in your planter so what I did  with that one is I have actually only  watered it once I wanted it when I  planted it to make sure that it was salt  and moist and this has grown in just  about like three days now I believe it  has grown quite quickly so at this point  now I can take it outside and replant it  now garlic though does take a few months  to grow so you're gonna have to be  patient with that one this next one I  have not done yet because we just moved  into this house so I didn't have a  garden yet but I wanted to still show  you because we have done it in the past  and it works great is you take a sweet  potato or a potato whichever one you  have around your house and these little  spots here these little nodes  they're called eyes so what you want to  do with these is you can cut it into  pieces I mean you can plant the whole  potato if you want to but you don't have  to do that you can plant in different  sections you have to make sure that you  cut at least one eye per each area that  you're planting I prefer to do two or  three now what you do with that is you  have to cut it into pieces and this part  the little eye section has to go upwards  because that is what's going to be the  growth it's gonna create the plant for  you I went ahead and cut this one just  so that you could see but I cannot use  this one you have to use ones that  already have the little sprouts growing  on them you know you've had those the  ones that you've forgot you have  potatoes forgot to cook them and then  started growing so those are perfect  you basically have to cut a few now if I  were to plant it in the ground I would  have to make sure to cut it a few inches  deep and these little nodes facing  upwards I am by no means an expert in  planting this is something new that I'm  trying out now that we've moved into our  own home so be patient if it doesn't  work out in the beginning sometimes we  he'll plants because we just love them  too much and we overwater them or we  forget and we underwater them and that  is okay the thing is you tried there are  already scraps so if you waste them you  were gonna throw them away anyway so try  it out it might surprise you and you  might have new veggies to share with  others  [Music]
 

